VERDICT: PASS
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: T6
ATTEMPT: 4
---
Scope/discipline (attack 3):
- `git diff HEAD --name-only`: .swarm/ledger.tsv, README.md, internal/config/config.go,
  internal/handlers/backup/handlers.go, internal/services/storage/migration.go,
  internal/services/storage/storage.go. The last four .go files were diffed line-by-line
  filtering out comment (`//`) and blank lines: zero non-comment lines changed in any of
  them — the whole diff across all four files, cumulative from attempts 1-4, is comments
  only. Attempt 4's own contribution (per manifest) is README.md (+2 lines) and
  migration.go (+5 lines, one comment block); confirmed via `git diff HEAD --
  internal/services/storage/migration.go` and `-- README.md`.
- `gofmt -l` on all four .go files: clean (no output).
- `go build ./...`: BUILD_OK. `go vet ./...`: VET_OK.

Attack 1 — false-claim check on the new "can X or Y" wording:
(a) "a save can replace the link with a regular file [target goes stale]" — verified via
    storage.atomicWrite (storage.go:582-609): stages to a temp file in the same dir, then
    os.Rename onto `path`; rename unlinks whatever is at `path` (symlink or not) and
    installs the new inode, so a pre-existing symlink is destroyed and its old target
    receives no further writes. Claim holds; not refuted.
(b) "a save can write straight through it, silently changing the file it points to" —
    verified two live sites: HandlePlotly's bare os.WriteFile(cachePath, ...) at
    handlers.go (write-through cache save, no user-facing error on success) and
    migration.go's two bare os.WriteFile calls (verifyPath at line ~48, markerPath at
    line ~103) inside EnableEncryptionWithProvider, reachable from POST
    /encryption/enable via HandleEnableEncryption / HandleEnableEncryptionWithMethod
    (handlers.go:110,590,871,917,958,1003). Checked whether the write-through is actually
    surfaced as an error, which would falsify "silently": on success neither path returns
    or logs any error to the caller/user — HandleEnableEncryption only logs+errors on
    failure of EnableEncryption itself, not on a successful write that happened to land
    through a symlink. "Silently" holds; not refuted.
(c) Per-comment factual checks against code:
    - SaveUserSettings (config.go): `grep -rn "SaveUserSettings(" --include=*.go .` finds
      only the function definition — still zero live call sites, comment's claim holds.
    - HandlePlotly (handlers.go): confirmed the write it describes is the bare
      os.WriteFile(cachePath, data, 0644) immediately below the comment, matches.
    - migration.go's comment claims to cover "here and at the marker write below": both
      verifyPath (`.encryption-verify`, const verifyFile) and markerPath (`.encrypted`,
      const markerFile) writes are bare os.WriteFile calls — confirmed both sites, comment
      accurately describes both.
    - atomicWrite: confirmed stage-in-same-dir via os.CreateTemp + os.Rename, i.e.
      genuinely stage-and-rename.

Attack 2 — omission check under the non-exhaustive wording:
- uploads' createExclusive (storage.go createExclusive, os.Link+EEXIST): if a symlink
  already sits at the upload destination, os.Link fails with os.ErrExist. Traced the only
  two callers: uploadOneFile (explorer/handlers.go:931) reports outcome
  {Status:"skipped", Reason:"already exists"} back to the client — a third behavior
  (refuse, loudly, no data touched) distinct from both documented modes. Judged against
  the task's own standard ("only a FAIL if a careful reader would be actively misled, not
  merely un-informed"): this third mode is strictly safer than either documented failure
  mode (no silent corruption, no stale target, error surfaced to the user) and does not
  contradict the README's imperative "don't point symlinks into or out of data/" — if
  anything it's consistent with "don't do this, it won't work right." Not misleading;
  recorded as an observation, not a FAIL.
- Symlinked directories (e.g. data/subdir -> elsewhere): writes through a symlinked
  parent honour the link. The warning clause "Don't point symlinks into or out of
  `data/`" is worded at the directory-boundary level (not "files" specifically) and a
  symlinked subdirectory is naturally read as a symlink "out of data/" — covered by the
  imperative even though the preceding sentence's two examples are phrased in terms of
  files. Borderline, but not actively misleading: the rule given still correctly tells
  the reader what not to do for this case too.

All three attack vectors failed to produce a false claim or an actively misleading
omission. PASS.
